DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     These interfaces may be used to look up information from the
     audit_user(5) database, which describes per-user audit configuration.
     Audit user entries are described by a _a_u___u_s_e_r___e_n_t, which stores the
     user's name in _a_u___n_a_m_e, events to always audit in _a_u___a_l_w_a_y_s, and events
     never to audit _a_u___n_e_v_e_r.

     The ggeettaauuuusseerreenntt() function returns the next user found in the
     audit_user(5) database, or the first if the function has not yet been
     called.  NULL will be returned if no further records are available.

     The ggeettaauuuusseerrnnaamm() function looks up a user by name.  NULL will be re‐
     turned if no matching class can be found.

     The sseettaauuuusseerr() function resets the iterator through the audit_user(5)
     database, causing the next call to ggeettaauuuusseerreenntt() to start again from the
     beginning of the file.

     The eennddaauuuusseerr() function closes the audit_user(5) database, if open.

     The aauu__uusseerr__mmaasskk() function calculates a new session audit mask to be re‐
     turned via _m_a_s_k___p for the user identified by _u_s_e_r_n_a_m_e.  If the user audit
     configuration is not found, the default system audit properties returned
     by getacflg(3) are used.  The resulting mask may be set via a call to
     setaudit(2) or related variants.

     The ggeettffaauuddiittffllaaggss() function generates a new process audit state by com‐
     bining the audit masks passed as parameters with the system audit masks.

